          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Clogged Gutters Are a Real Problem, Not Just an Eyesore
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gutters exist to move water away from your home's foundation, walls, and roofline in a controlled way. When leaves, needles, and debris build up and block that flow, water backs up instead — pooling on the roof deck where it can work under shingles, overflowing the gutter's edge and running down siding and fascia boards, or simply saturating the soil right next to your foundation where it's specifically not supposed to go. Left unaddressed over multiple seasons, this pattern is directly linked to <strong>rotted fascia boards, ice dams in cold climates, foundation cracking, and even interior water damage</strong> when backed-up water finds its way under roofing material.
          </p>

          <p>
            The other cost of clogged gutters is more mundane but very real: cleaning them yourself means a ladder, wet debris, and a genuinely unpleasant task most homeowners put off far longer than they should. Hiring it out typically runs <strong>$150-300 per cleaning visit</strong>, and most homes need that done twice a year. A quality gutter guard system doesn't eliminate maintenance entirely, but it dramatically reduces both the frequency of cleaning and the real structural risk of a clog you didn't catch in time. Multiply that twice-a-year professional cleaning cost out over the ten-plus-year lifespan of a good gutter system, and the math behind investing in guards upfront becomes hard to ignore.
          </p>

