          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Gutter Guards Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            The <strong>best gutter guard</strong> for your home depends on the trees around your property, your roof pitch, your gutter profile, and how much installation effort you're willing to put in. Here's a breakdown of the four main categories:
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Micro-Mesh Guards</h3>
          <p>
            The most comprehensive protection available, using a fine metal mesh that blocks nearly everything — leaves, pine needles, and even shingle grit — while still passing water through effectively. This is the <strong>best gutter guard for leaves and pine needles combined</strong>, and the category most professionals recommend for genuinely low-maintenance, long-term protection. It requires more involved installation than simpler options but delivers the strongest overall results.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reverse Curve (Surface Tension) Guards</h3>
          <p>
            Also called "helmet" style guards, these solid covers use water's tendency to cling to a curved surface, pulling rainwater into the gutter while debris slides off the front edge to the ground. There's no mesh to ever clog, making this a strong <strong>low-maintenance gutter guard</strong> option, but performance depends heavily on matching the guard's curve angle to your specific roof pitch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foam Inserts</h3>
          <p>
            Foam wedges that sit directly inside the gutter channel, filtering debris on the surface while water passes through the foam's internal structure. This is the <strong>easiest gutter guard to install yourself</strong> — no tools, brackets, or roof access required — and performs especially well against pine needles, though the foam itself needs periodic replacement every few years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Brush Inserts</h3>
          <p>
            Cylindrical bristle brushes that sit inside the gutter, catching larger debris on top while water flows underneath. The simplest system to install and remove, and forgiving of irregular or slightly damaged gutters, but less effective than mesh against fine debris and requires periodic manual clearing of trapped material.
          </p>

