          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes When Buying and Installing Gutter Guards
          </h2>

          <p>
            Gutter guards are a genuinely worthwhile investment, but the wrong choice or a rushed installation can undermine the whole point. These are the most frequent mistakes we see.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Installing Over Dirty Gutters</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Putting any gutter guard on top of existing debris just seals the problem in place rather than solving it. Always start with a full gutter cleaning before installing guards of any type, and confirm downspouts are flowing freely before you close everything up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Reverse-Curve Guards on an Incompatible Roof Pitch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Surface-tension guards depend on a specific relationship between water flow and the curve angle. Installed on the wrong roof pitch, water can overshoot the gutter entirely during heavy rain instead of curving in as designed. Confirm your roof pitch is within the manufacturer's recommended range before choosing this style.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating Pine Needle Debris</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard mesh handles typical leaf debris well but can let fine pine needles through, especially as mesh openings gradually widen with age and weather exposure. If your property has heavy pine cover, invest in a tighter-weave mesh or foam insert specifically suited to needles rather than a standard leaf-focused guard.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Hose Test After Installation</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gutter guard that looks properly installed can still have a gap, incorrect angle, or seam issue that only shows up under real water flow. Running a hose along the roofline immediately after installation catches these problems while you're still set up with a ladder, rather than discovering an overflow issue during the next storm.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

